<%# The one place an administrator moves a student's money by hand.

    It was the second half of `_form`, in a card headed "Add portfolio transaction" under a card headed
    "Student details". The record page's section heading names it now, so the card carries no header of
    its own - the same treatment as every other form on this shape.

    **Rendered only when the student has a portfolio.** It used to render regardless, reading
    `portfolio&.cash_balance || 0` for its display and posting to `add_transaction`, which builds a
    `PortfolioTransaction` with a nil portfolio - a `belongs_to` - so every submit failed validation and
    redirected with an alert. A control whose only possible outcome is an error is not a control.

    **No read-only "Current cash balance" line.** The header's summary states the balance, and on a merged
    page this block would have been the third copy of one number: summary line, figure tile, and here.

    **Bound to `CashAdjustment`**, so each field carries its own error and a rejected submit keeps what was
    typed. The four fields were loose params reported by a redirect with `alert:`, which put the message at
    the top of the page and emptied the form on the way. See the class for what that could not validate. %>
<%= form_with model: adjustment,
              url: add_transaction_admin_student_path(student), method: :post,
              builder: Ui::FormBuilder, class: "space-y-6" do |f| %>
  <%= render "shared/form_errors", record: adjustment, subject: "transaction" %>

  <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
    <div>
      <%= f.select :transaction_type,
                   CashAdjustment::DIRECTIONS.map { |direction| [direction.humanize, direction] },
                   label: "Transaction type",
                   width: :half,
                   required: true,
                   hint: "Deposit adds to the cash balance, debit takes from it",
                   include_blank: "Select a transaction type" %>

      <%# A text field with `inputmode`, not `number_field`. GOV.UK's money pattern is explicit about this:
          a number input carries spinners, changes value on a stray scroll, and silently rejects a pasted
          "$12.50". The keypad on a phone is the same either way. %>
      <%= f.text_field :amount,
                       label: "Amount",
                       width: :half,
                       required: true,
                       hint: "In dollars, like 12.50",
                       placeholder: "0.00",
                       inputmode: "decimal",
                       autocomplete: "off" %>

      <%= f.select :reason,
                   CashAdjustment::REASONS.map { |key| [PortfolioTransaction.human_attribute_name("reason.#{key}"), key] },
                   label: "Reason",
                   width: :half,
                   required: true,
                   hint: "Where this money came from, or what it was for",
                   include_blank: "Select a reason" %>

      <%= f.text_area :description,
                      label: "Description (optional)",
                      hint: "Notes for other administrators - the student never sees this.",
                      placeholder: "Enter transaction details...",
                      rows: 3 %>
    </div>
    <% end %>

  <%# Not `tw-form-actions`: that class hides an update form's row until a field changes and then sticks
      it to the bottom of the viewport. This form *creates* something, so there is nothing to compare
      against and nothing to reveal - and two sticky rows on one page would fight. %>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
    <%= f.submit_button "Add transaction", variant: :secondary %>
  </div>
<% end %>
